---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Showcase from "../../components/Showcase.astro";
import Used from "../../components/Used.astro";

import "../../examples/state-layer.css";
import listSource from "../../examples/state-layer.css?raw";

const usage = `
<!-- No extra imports: the state layer comes in tokens.scss.
     You just mark the item. -->
<button class="sl-tile sk-interactive" type="button">
  Interactive
</button>`;

const projectedUsage = `<!-- resulting DOM; it is written by the adapter. -->
<div
  class="sk-combobox__item sk-interactive"
  data-sk-combobox-item
  data-highlighted
  role="option"
>
  Argentina
</div>`;

const bareHtml = `<div class="sl-tiles">
  <button class="sl-tile sk-interactive" type="button">
    Interactivo
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    data-peek="hover"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Hover
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    data-peek="focus"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Focus
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    data-peek="pressed"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Pressed
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    aria-selected="true"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Selected
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    data-dragging="true"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Dragged
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    aria-disabled="true"
    disabled
  >
    Disabled
  </button>
</div>`;

const currentColorHtml = `<div class="sl-tiles">
  <button
    class="sl-tile sl-current-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
  >
    Interactivo
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sl-current-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    data-peek="hover"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Hover
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sl-current-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    data-peek="focus"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Focus
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sl-current-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    data-peek="pressed"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Pressed
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sl-current-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    aria-selected="true"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Selected
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sl-current-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    data-dragging="true"
    tabindex="-1"
  >
    Dragged
  </button>
  <button
    class="sl-tile sl-current-tile sk-interactive"
    type="button"
    aria-disabled="true"
    disabled
  >
    Disabled
  </button>
</div>`;

const labHtml = `<div class="sl-lab">
  <p class="sl-lab__caption">
    Superficie neutra · hover, foco, press, selected, disabled
  </p>
  <nav class="sl-list" aria-label="Carpetas">
    <a class="sl-item sk-interactive" href="#">
      <span data-sk-icon="info"></span>
      <span class="sl-item__label">inbox</span>
      <span class="sl-item__meta">128</span>
    </a>
    <a
      class="sl-item sk-interactive"
      href="#"
      aria-selected="true"
    >
      <span data-sk-icon="check"></span>
      <span class="sl-item__label">Enviados</span>
      <span class="sl-item__meta">12</span>
    </a>
    <a class="sl-item sk-interactive" href="#">
      <span data-sk-icon="edit"></span>
      <span class="sl-item__label">Borradores</span>
      <span class="sl-item__meta">3</span>
    </a>
    <a
      class="sl-item sk-interactive"
      href="#"
      aria-disabled="true"
    >
      <span data-sk-icon="settings"></span>
      <span class="sl-item__label">Archivados</span>
    </a>
  </nav>

  <p class="sl-lab__caption">
    Saturated fill · layer is currentColor (on-accent)
  </p>
  <div class="sl-actions">
    <button
      class="sk-button sk-interactive"
      data-sk-button
      data-variant="primary"
      type="button"
    >
      Publicar
    </button>
    <button
      class="sk-button sk-interactive"
      data-sk-button
      type="button"
    >
      Cancel
    </button>
    <button
      class="sk-button sk-interactive"
      data-sk-button
      data-variant="ghost"
      type="button"
      disabled
    >
      Deshacer
    </button>
  </div>
</div>`;

const ladder = [
  ["disabled", "--state-layer-disabled-opacity", "force layer to 0"],
  ["dragged", "--state-layer-dragged-opacity", ""],
  ["pressed", "--state-layer-pressed-opacity", ""],
  ["focus", "--state-layer-focus-opacity", ""],
  ["hover", "--state-layer-hover-opacity", ""],
  ["selected", "--state-layer-selected-opacity", "the base step"],
];
---

<Base
  title="State layer"
  description="Two ways to connect the same feedback: direct DOM states and machine-projected states."
>
  <h1>State layer</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The same semi-transparent overlay expresses hover, focus, pressed, selected and dragged. The system
    You have two ways to connect it: from the real state of the DOM or from the logical state of a machine.
    They share paint and tokens; They are not two visual variants nor two superimposed layers.
  </p>

  <h2>Two connection types, single layer</h2>
  <p>
    The type describes <strong>who knows the state</strong>. If the element itself receives hover,
    focus and press, the connection is direct. If DOM focus remains on another node and a machine knows
    which item is active, the adapter projects that state to the DOM.
  </p>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Tipo</th><th>source of truth</th><th>use it when</th><th>Who connects it</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Directo</strong></td>
          <td><code>:hover</code>, <code>:focus-visible</code>, <code>:active</code>, ARIA y <code>:has()</code></td>
          <td>The same button, link, tile or wrapper has the interaction.</td>
          <td>The consumer adds <code>sk-interactive</code> once.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Proyectado</strong></td>
          <td><code>data-highlighted</code>, <code>data-selected</code>, <code>data-disabled</code> and other machine states.</td>
          <td>A composite widget retains focus on its input or root, but activates an internal item.</td>
          <td>The adapter and the CSS of the component; the consumer does not recreate the mapping.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>1. Direct: the element has the state</h2>
  <p>
    Button, link and tile receive the browser states in the same node that is painted. The pattern
    comes in <code>tokens.scss</code>: the consumer only adds <code>sk-interactive</code>. Your
    Selectors also read ARIA and use <code>:has()</code> when a wrapper contains the native input.
    The <code>::before</code> remains inert in everything else.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={usage} label="HTML · direct connection" note="mark the target once" />

  <h3>All states, two surfaces</h3>
  <p>
    The two previews freeze the same entire staircase to compare it without moving the pointer.
    The first uses a neutral surface; the second, an inverse surface whose layer inherits
    <code>currentColor</code>. In both, the first tile is still interactive.
  </p>
  <Showcase html={bareHtml} label="Neutral surface · all states" />
  <Showcase
    html={currentColorHtml}
    label="currentColor · all states"
  />
  <p>
    Selected, hover, focus, pressed and dragged now advance in visible steps. In high contrast
    the wash does not increase: doing so would bring the background closer to the text and dull the surface. Instead,
    A keyline inset appears whose thickness grows with each state.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Projected: the machine owns the state</h2>
  <p>
    In Combobox, Menu, DatePicker and TreeView the focus can remain on the input or the root while
    The arrows activate another item. This item does not necessarily coincide with
    <code>:focus-visible</code>. The adapter writes <code>data-highlighted</code>,
    <code>data-selected</code> or <code>data-disabled</code>, and the component's CSS translates that
    logical state to the same opacity tokens.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    code={projectedUsage}
    label="Resulting DOM · projected connection"
    note="it is written by the adapter; not copied"
  />
  <div class="sk-alert" data-tone="info" data-presentation="accent">
    <div class="sk-alert__content">
      <p>
        <strong>The consumer does not choose between two classes.</strong> Uses <code>sk-interactive</code>
        for your own DOM target. When consuming a composite widget, its adapter and stylesheet are already
        They have the projected connection.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Mismo layer, superficies distintas</h2>
  <p>
    Neutral surface and saturated fill are not additional types: they only change the
    <code>currentColor</code> that dyes the same layer. The list uses the text color; on the button
    primary veil is <code>on-accent</code>. Change brand, mode or contrast: the contract is maintained.
  </p>
  <Showcase html={labHtml} label="Lista + acciones" />

  <h2>The dye is <code>currentColor</code></h2>
  <p>
    The color of the layer is <Used token="--state-layer-color" />, the content color of the layer itself
    component. A single token covers all cases:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Subsume negro-sobre-clear / blanco-sobre-dark:</strong> A neutral surface has dark (clear) or clear (dark) text, and the layer darkens or lightens it alone, without a branch per mode.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Y is correct over saturated fills</strong>, where setting black or white fails: the
      content of a primary button uses <code>on-accent</code>, which resolves based on padding.
      The layer inherits that exact tone instead of assuming one.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Zero color tokens per component.</strong> One fits all.</li>
  </ul>


  <h2>Exactly an active layer</h2>
  <p>
    The two connections write a single opacity over a single pseudo-element; They never stack overlays.
    In direct connection, the shared pattern resolves simultaneous matches with this ladder,
    From highest to lowest priority:
  </p>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Prioridad</th><th>Status</th><th>Opacidad usada</th><th></th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          ladder.map(([state, token, note], i) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="count">{i + 1}</td>
              <td class="mono">{state}</td>
              <td><Used token={token} /></td>
              <td class="count">{note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="sk-alert" data-tone="info" data-presentation="accent">
    <div class="sk-alert__content">
    <p>
      <strong>The ladder does not use combined selectors.</strong> Each state rule assigns the
      <em>same</em> <code>--state-layer-opacity</code>, and all selectors have the same
      specificity (0,2,0), so when several match, it decides the <strong>order in the
      file</strong>. The rules are written in ascending priority: last one wins.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>In direct connection, <code>selected</code> is the lowest step</h2>
  <p>
    Una base persistente debajo de hover significaría dos capas superpuestas, es decir, opacidad
    sumada, que el modelo prohíbe. Ayes que selected es el más bajo y hover/pressed lo reemplazan.
  </p>
  <p>
    The selection never disappears because <strong>always carries a separate flag</strong>: a
    check, a filled control, a rail, a type weight. In the list above, "Sent" has a
    accent rail and emphasized typography, does not depend on the layer. If you need one more selected+hover
    strong, you define a combined opacity token; never a sum in runtime.
  </p>

  <h2>Accesibilidad, no negociable</h2>
  <p>The layer <strong> is never the only signal</strong>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The spotlight has an independent ring (<Used token="--focus-ring-width" /> of
      <Used token="--focus-ring-color" />), verified at ≥3:1 by the validator. That is why he lives in his
      own family <code>--focus-ring-*</code> and not within <code>--state-layer-*</code>: the name
      charge the warranty.
    </li>
    <li>The selection carries a real indicator.</li>
    <li>
      <code>disabled</code> fades with the component tokens and <strong>forces the layer to 0</strong>.
    </li>
    <li><code>:focus-visible</code> is used, not <code>:focus</code>, so a mouse click does not leave a layer stuck.</li>
    <li>The fade respects <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>High contrast adds a keyline, no more wash</h2>
  <p>
    Light and dark share the same ladder: <code>currentColor</code> already changes with the mode.
    High contrast also does not duplicate opacities. That would bring the background closer to the text color and make
    that the control would read off. <code>[data-contrast="high"]</code> preserves the wash, adds a
    keyline inset proportional to the state and increases the focus ring to <code>3px</code>. Active
    high contrast above and compare both galleries.
  </p>

  <h2>Your CSS</h2>
  <p>
    For your own DOM target, the pattern sends the structure of the layer and the ladder; you write the
    layout and add the class. Composite widgets already send their mapping projected in the CSS of the
    component:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={listSource} label="src/examples/state-layer.css" note="the CSS of the previews ↞" />

  <p>
    Zero state colors and zero hardcoded opacities. Use direct connection on your own controls;
    for machine states, it consumes the component instead of duplicating its internal selectors.
  </p>

  <div class="sk-alert" data-tone="info" data-presentation="accent">
    <div class="sk-alert__content">
    <p>
      A consumer with a more specific selector than <code>.sk-interactive:hover</code> can break
      the staircase It is a CSS limitation, the same one you accept
      <a href="/en/styling-hooks">the styling hooks contract</a>.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
